In article <1992Feb12.032845.11148@kurango.cit.gu.edu.au> anthony@kurango.cit.gu.edu.au (Anthony Thyssen) writes:
>andra@cs.widener.edu (Robert Andracsek) writes:
>|The WoW can be recharged once.  After that, when you try to charge it,
>|it will explode.  So in effect, the wand can be good for up to seven
>|wishes, one of them for three blessed scrolls of charging, which can
>|be erased and used again.
>
>Not counting the wish for charging scrolls how do you arrange for
>6 normal wishes from a WoW. How many charges are you assuming the WoW
>has to begin with. I've seen wands that have only one charge (found in
>the castle).
>
>  I see this 6 wish thing mentioned in this group before and even mentioned
>in the NetHack Spoilers. But it has never been explained properly, and
>I have not had an opertunity to try it myself.

Simple really.  You start with a WoW with 1 or more wishes.  You wish for 
'3 blessed scrolls of charging'.  Continue wishing until it has 0 charges.
Read one of the scrolls on it. Continue wishing (or trying to) until the wand
sorta disintegrates.
If the WoW is found with 0 or -1 charges on it, and you already have a blessed
scroll of charging, you can get three or four wishes out of it, respectively.
If it has 3 charges (the usual for the Castle wand), you get 6 wishes, 7 if you
already have a scroll of charging.

